abs_parts = [
 "Background: Compromised peer review, rogue editors and paper mills threaten research integrity, and unusually short times from submission to acceptance are widely cited as a warning sign. Article-level evidence that adjusts for journal and year is lacking.",
 f"Methods: We linked publisher-deposited received and accepted dates of {BS['n_articles']:,} articles in 800 Springer Nature, Wiley and Taylor & Francis journals to the Retraction Watch database (snapshot of 24 November 2026). The primary exposure was acceptance within the fastest 5% of the same journal's articles in the same acceptance year; the primary outcome was retraction for peer-review-related reasons (compromised peer review, rogue editor, paper mill, third-party involvement). We used Mantel–Haenszel odds ratios, fixed-effects logistic and stratified Cox models within journal-by-year strata, a journal-level random-effects meta-analysis, a negative-control exposure and an out-of-time score evaluated on articles accepted in 2022–2024. The protocol was fixed beforehand; amendments are disclosed (RECORD).",
 f"Results: {BS['n_retracted']:,} articles were retracted, {BS['n_process']:,} for peer-review-related reasons. The fastest 5% had {f1(x1['or_mh'])} times the odds of a peer-review-related retraction (journal-cluster 95% CI {f1(x1['ci_cluster'][0])}–{f1(x1['ci_cluster'][1])}; adjusted {f1(j['X1']['or_'])}; hazard ratio {f1(cxp['X1']['hr'])}) and the slowest 5% {f2(x2['or_mh'])} times, with a graded dose-response; a negative-control exposure was null (OR {f2(n1['or_mh'])}; cluster CI {f2(n1['ci_cluster'][0])}–{f2(n1['ci_cluster'][1])}). The odds ratio was {f1(a17['month']['or_mh'])} within journal-months, {f1(a15['R_nopub']['or_mh'])} for retractions not labelled as publisher investigations, and {f1(a4['excl_mass_X1_process']['or_mh'])} after removing mass-retraction journal-years. It was heterogeneous: pooled across {a16['n_journals']} journals {f1(a16['pooled_or'])} (I² {100*a16['I2']:.0f}%; prediction interval {f1(a16['prediction_interval'][0])}–{f1(a16['prediction_interval'][1])}), and by acceptance year {f1(yr['2020']['or_mh'])} (2020), {f1(yr['2021']['or_mh'])} (2021), {f1(yr['2022']['or_mh'])} (2022), {f1(yr['2023']['or_mh'])} (2023) and {f2(yr['2024']['or_mh'])} (2024). An out-of-time score had a stratified AUC of {f2(a18['auc_all']['auc'])} ({f2(a18['auc_all']['ci'][0])}–{f2(a18['auc_all']['ci'][1])}), {f2(a18['auc_excl_top5']['auc'])} without the five journals with most events; among the fastest 1%, {pc(sc['pit_le_0.01']['ppv'], 2)} were retracted against {pc(sc['base_rate'], 2)} overall, at {pc(sc['pit_le_0.01']['sensitivity'], 0)} sensitivity.",
 "Conclusions: Acceptance that is unusually fast for the same journal and year is associated with later retraction for peer-review-related reasons, but the association varies widely between journals and years and has weakened for recent articles. As a screen it is weak, since most flagged articles are not retracted; it may help prioritise editorial-office review of groups of articles but must not be used to judge individual papers."]
