# ------------------------------------------------------------------ background
H("Background")
P("Retractions are the most visible consequence of failures of research integrity, and a substantial share involve failures of the editorial process itself: compromised or fabricated peer review, editors who accept manuscripts they should not, and paper mills that place manuscripts through compromised guest-edited collections ⟦candal2022_bmj,parker2024_jce,copestm2022_paper_mills⟧. Publishers and integrity groups list unusually short times from submission to acceptance among the signs that a manuscript may not have been properly reviewed ⟦copestm2022_paper_mills,parker2024_jce⟧, yet the empirical support for this widely repeated rule is thin.")
P("Existing evidence points in the same direction but is limited in design. Special-issue articles are accepted faster and more uniformly than regular articles ⟦hanson2024_qss,bishop2023_hindawi_si,petrou,gleasner2025_learnpubl,mills2025_accres⟧, and retracted AI-related special-issue articles were accepted faster than other retracted ones ⟦sridharan2026_frma⟧. Studies of editors found that those handling papers later flagged on PubPeer or retracted had shorter response times ⟦bishop2023_hindawi_si,richardson2025_pnas⟧, with short turnaround described as a less robust marker at PLOS ONE ⟦richardson2025_pnas⟧; editor–author relations also shape handling times ⟦sarigol2017_scientometrics⟧. At journal level, lag has been used as a red flag for nepotism ⟦scanff2021_plosbiol⟧, while journals with many paper-mill retractions did not differ significantly in their reported editorial times ⟦mascato2025_ripr⟧. Descriptive work on COVID-19 articles reported very short acceptance times alongside retractions ⟦schonhaut2022_rpsp,horbach2020_qss⟧ and median review times of retracted papers ⟦kocyigit2023_jkms⟧. Studies in this journal have mapped retraction reasons and notices with Retraction Watch data ⟦coi_notices2026,cochrane_retracted2026,notice_type2026⟧, and machine-learning models of retraction have not used timing features ⟦fletcher2025_ripr⟧. Recent work has shown that retraction labels are revised over time ⟦singh2026_rw_revisions⟧ and has used journal-by-year strata with a frozen reference date for another retraction predictor ⟦arjomandi2026_arxiv⟧. We found no earlier study that estimates, for individual articles, whether acceptance that is unusually fast for the same journal and year is followed by retraction, separates retraction reasons, and tests a score out of time, nor any study of same-day batch acceptance, weekend acceptance or accepted-to-online lag as retraction predictors; our searches did not cover Scopus, Web of Science, Dimensions or publishers' internal analyses.")
P("Three problems make a naive comparison of retracted and other articles misleading. Journals differ greatly in speed and in retraction risk, so differences between journals and years must be removed; retractions accumulate with follow-up time, so articles must be compared within the same acceptance period; and retraction labels are heterogeneous, so a signal specific to the editorial process should differ between process-related and other reasons. A further problem is that publishers may have used turnaround time to find the articles they retracted, which would make an association partly circular. We therefore ask: (1) within a journal and year, is unusually fast acceptance associated with later retraction for peer-review-related reasons, and is the relation graded and specific to such reasons? (2) Is it robust to finer time strata, mass-retraction events, journals with most events and publisher-led ascertainment, and how heterogeneous is it across journals and years? (3) How well does a score computed only from a journal's earlier articles identify articles that are later retracted, and at what cost in false positives? (4) Do same-day batch acceptance, weekend acceptance and accepted-to-online lag add information? The study is observational and does not address whether any individual paper was properly reviewed.")
# ------------------------------------------------------------------ methods
H("Methods")
H("Design, reporting and pre-specification", 2)
P("This is a retrospective cohort study of published articles with retraction as the outcome, reported according to the RECORD extension of the STROBE statement for routinely collected data ⟦record2015,strobe2007⟧ (checklist in the supplement). The protocol, including exposures, outcomes, analyses and hypotheses, was committed to the project's git history before any association between timing and retraction was computed; this is a pre-specification by the analysts, not a registered report, and no external time stamp exists. Before the protocol we looked only at counts of retractions and reason labels. After the first results we added analyses (amendments E-A1 to E-A6): a numerical correction of the optimiser, a negative-control and reason-subtype analysis, external replication, equal-follow-up and event-concentration analyses, accepted-to-online lag, a stratified Cox model, and, after an independent review of the first complete results, a correction of the cohort and further analyses of ascertainment, between-journal heterogeneity, finer strata, screening robustness and year heterogeneity. Each amendment was written before the analysis it describes was run and is marked as post hoc where it responds to results. Results with the first (cycle-1) cohort are kept in the repository and summarised below.")
H("Data and cohort", 2)
P(f"Articles were the research articles of 800 journals of Springer Nature, Wiley and Taylor & Francis whose received and accepted dates are deposited in Crossref (journals with at least 80% dated records and 150–3,000 dated articles in 2023, selected from the 1,000 journals with most articles per publisher). We started from {BS['flow'][0]['n']:,} Crossref records with unique DOIs, and excluded records without parsable dates, with a duration below 1 or above 1,460 days, with acceptance more than seven days after a true first-online date where one exists, and with titles indicating non-research items; no exclusion depended on retraction status or on reference lists, and {BS['n_articles']:,} articles remained (Table 1). A first version of the cohort used the cycle-1 cleaning rules, which removed 28% of retracted articles because a fallback date replaced the first-online date in journals without one; this was corrected (amendment E-A6). Median time from received to accepted was {T['D_quantiles']['0.5']:.0f} days (5th to 95th percentile {T['D_quantiles']['0.05']:.0f}–{T['D_quantiles']['0.95']:.0f}). Retractions came from the Retraction Watch database in the file distributed by Crossref (snapshot downloaded 24 November 2026, SHA-256 in the repository) ⟦rwdb⟧, matched on the DOI of the original article. A retraction was a record of nature 'Retraction'; expressions of concern and corrections were not counted, and DOIs with a reinstatement record were treated as not retracted. Reasons are semicolon-separated labels; because labels are revised over time ⟦singh2026_rw_revisions⟧, the snapshot is pinned and the process-related set was defined by substrings that cover renamed labels.")
H("Outcomes", 2)
P("R_any was any retraction. R_process was a retraction with a reason containing 'peer review' (which covers compromised peer review, concerns about peer review and fake peer review), 'rogue editor', 'paper mill' or 'concerns/issues about third party involvement'. R_other was a retraction that was not R_process. R_ai was a retraction for computer-aided or computer-generated content (exploratory). To examine circular ascertainment we also defined retractions whose reasons did not include 'investigation by journal/publisher' (R_nopub) and those that included 'investigation by third party'.")
H("Exposures", 2)
P("All primary exposures use only received and accepted dates. The primary exposure X1 was a time from received to accepted at or below the 5th percentile of the journal × acceptance-year stratum (strata with at least 100 articles); X2 was the corresponding 95th-percentile flag for slow acceptance. Secondary exposures were acceptance within 7 days (X3), same-day batch acceptance (at least 5 or at least 10 articles of the journal accepted on the same calendar day, X4 and X4b), acceptance on a weekend (X5), and a short or long accepted-to-first-online lag within the stratum for articles with a true first-online date (L1, L2). As a negative control we used receipt on a weekend (N1), which has no plausible link to later retraction. For prospective screening we computed an out-of-time score: the probability integral transform (PIT) of the article's duration under a forecast built only from the same journal's articles that were already accepted and online in the 12 months before the quarter of receipt, a Gaussian-smoothed empirical distribution whose bandwidth includes a cohort-shift variance estimated earlier in this project (code in the repository); low values mean faster than the journal's recent history. The covariate was the logarithm of the number of authors; reference counts were not used because the reference lists of retracted records are unreliable (median 31 against 42 references).")
H("Statistical analysis", 2)
P(f"The primary analysis compared exposed and unexposed articles within journal × acceptance-year strata ({BS['n_strata']:,} strata), which removes differences between journals and years and equalises follow-up, using Mantel–Haenszel odds ratios ⟦mantel1959,robins1986⟧ with journal-cluster bootstrap intervals (300–500 draws, resampling journals) ⟦efron1979,field2007⟧, and a fixed-effects logistic regression with stratum dummies and journal-clustered sandwich standard errors that included X1, X2, X4, X5 and the logarithm of authors jointly. A stratified Cox model for the time from acceptance to retraction (censoring at the data cut) used the same covariates and journal-cluster bootstrap intervals ⟦cox1972⟧. The two primary tests (X1 with R_process and with R_any) were adjusted by the Holm method. Dose-response used 20 bins of the within-stratum percentile of duration. Heterogeneity across journals was examined by a random-effects meta-analysis of journal-specific Mantel–Haenszel odds ratios (journals with at least 10 process-related events; DerSimonian–Laird) ⟦dersimonian1986⟧, and across acceptance years by Cochran's Q. Residual confounding was examined with journal × quarter and journal × month strata. Prospective performance was assessed for articles accepted from 2022 to 2024 (at least 22 months of follow-up), with stratified and pooled areas under the receiver-operating-characteristic curve ⟦hanley1982⟧ (journal-cluster bootstrap), positive predictive value, lift, sensitivity and the number needed to screen, and with restrictions to 2023–2024, to journals with at least 80% true first-online dates, and excluding the five journals with most events. Robustness analyses removed journal-years with at least 25 retractions (mass-retraction events), removed R_ai, restricted to retractions within 24 months of acceptance, and removed the 1 to 20 journals with most events. We replicated the analysis in {a7['n_articles']:,} articles of {a7['n_journals']} journals of other publishers with PubMed dates (collected for an earlier cycle of this project). All code, protocol and data are in the repository.")
H("Use of AI", 2)
P("The analysis code, the figures and the first drafts of this manuscript were produced with an AI system (Claude, Anthropic) acting as an autonomous research assistant in a sandboxed environment; separately launched instances of the same model family performed literature searches, adversarial reviews that found errors (including the cohort problem above) and reproducibility audits. A previous version of this project was rejected by a journal partly because of the extent of AI use. The authors have re-examined the results and take responsibility for the content; the AI system is not an author.")
