# ------------------------------------------------------------------ discussion
H("Discussion")
P(f"In 1.8 million articles of three large publishers, acceptance that was unusually fast for the same journal and year was followed by retraction for peer-review-related reasons about four times as often as in the rest of the same journal-year. The relation was graded, specific to the editorial process, present for a time-to-event analysis, stable with finer time strata, absent for a negative-control exposure, and not explained by the journals with most events. This agrees in direction with, and extends to individual articles, the editor-level and special-issue-level observations made for Hindawi and PLOS ONE ⟦bishop2023_hindawi_si,richardson2025_pnas⟧ and with the descriptive findings for retracted papers ⟦kocyigit2023_jkms,schonhaut2022_rpsp,sridharan2026_frma⟧. The journal-level null result of a recent study in this journal ⟦mascato2025_ripr⟧ is not in conflict: our ecological analysis also found no positive relation for the share of very fast acceptances, and the signal is relative to the journal's own norm and visible at article level.")
P(f"The estimate nevertheless hides large differences. The association was strong in some journals and absent or reversed in others (prediction interval {f1(a16['prediction_interval'][0])}–{f1(a16['prediction_interval'][1])}), strong for articles accepted in 2020–2022 and not detectable in 2024, and the discriminatory performance of the out-of-time score fell from {f2(a18['auc_all']['auc'])} to {f2(a18['auc_excl_top5']['auc'])} when the five journals with most events were removed. Taken together with the strong concentration of events, this suggests that the average relation is dominated by a limited number of episodes in which compromised handling produced fast acceptance in bulk, probably in guest-edited collections, rather than a continuous gradient that holds everywhere. We cannot test that explanation here because article type and special-issue status are not recorded in the data. The decline in recent years may reflect manipulation that no longer produces unusually fast acceptances, detection and retraction that lag behind, or a change in what is retracted; screening rules should therefore be re-estimated routinely rather than fixed.")
P("The size of the effect and its practical value are different questions. The odds ratio is large, but retractions are rare, so flagged articles are overwhelmingly not retracted: even in the fastest 1% only about one in 150 articles was later retracted for process-related reasons, and the score identified a small share of such retractions. Timing is therefore not a detector of misconduct. A reasonable use, consistent with how publishers describe their own integrity screening, is to prioritise journals, special issues or editors for editorial-office review when a cluster of unusually fast acceptances occurs, in combination with other signals, and never to infer anything about a single paper. The companion tool in the repository computes within-journal-year percentiles, batch days and group-level alerts for this purpose and states these limits. The ascertainment problem deserves emphasis: publishers that screen by turnaround and then retract will generate an association even if turnaround says nothing about review quality. Our analyses without the publisher-investigation label and with third-party investigations gave similar estimates but are small, so the association should be read as one between fast acceptance and publisher-detected process failures, which is exactly what screening tools are asked to predict, but not as independent proof that fast acceptance indicates inadequate review.")
P("Three points of method may help later work. Pooling across journals and years, or comparing retracted with all other articles, would confound speed with journal practice and follow-up; the ecological association of very fast articles and retraction even has the opposite sign to the within-journal one. Cohort construction matters: a cleaning rule that looks neutral (excluding articles accepted after a first-online date that was in some journals only a month-level placeholder) removed 28% of retracted articles, and was found only by an independent re-analysis. And because a handful of journals contribute most events, associations such as that for batch acceptance can vanish when mass-retraction events are removed, so conclusions should always be checked for concentration. The article-level timing data used here are routinely deposited by publishers, and the analysis can be repeated by journals on their own submission data, where handling-editor and article-type information would sharpen it.")
H("Strengths and limitations")
for t in [f"Strengths: a very large cohort ({BS['n_articles']:,} articles, {BS['n_retracted']:,} retractions) with journal-by-year strata, a protocol fixed in advance with amendments disclosed, a negative-control exposure, a reason-specific outcome, a time-to-event analysis, an out-of-time score, a journal-level meta-analysis, and code and data in a public repository.",
          "Observational design: associations are conditional on journal and year; they do not show that fast acceptance caused inadequate review, and we cannot observe the review itself. Article type, special-issue status and handling editor are not available, so fast-lane items and guest-edited collections are not separated.",
          "Outcome: retraction is a late, incomplete and publisher-dependent marker; unretracted problematic articles are counted as controls, which would bias the odds ratio towards one if they are also fast; publishers may have used timing in their own investigations (circular ascertainment); Retraction Watch labels are revised over time ⟦singh2026_rw_revisions⟧ and the snapshot does not record when each notice entered the database.",
          f"Generality: the data are three publishers whose journals deposit received and accepted dates; Taylor & Francis contributed {T['by_member']['Taylor & Francis']['retracted']} retractions, so results speak for Springer Nature and Wiley journals; the Wiley odds ratio was smaller and its interval included one; the external replication had too few events to be informative; the association varies widely across journals and years.",
          "Concentration and inference: most events come from few journals and from articles accepted in 2020–2022; journal-cluster intervals rest on a limited number of influential clusters; the score's stratified AUC rests on a small number of outcome-selected journal-years and falls without the five journals with most events; the articles evaluated out of time are left-truncated because articles with long durations accepted after the origin are not yet in the data for the earliest years.",
          "Exposure: duration is publisher-defined and, for resubmitted or transferred manuscripts, may not measure the editorial process; 37% of articles have only a month-level issue date instead of a true first-online date, which affects the visibility rule of the score (a sensitivity analysis is reported) and the lag analysis (restricted to true dates); reference lists of retracted records are unreliable, so reference counts were not used.",
          "Multiplicity and post hoc analyses: only two tests were primary; amendments that responded to results are disclosed and should be read as exploratory.",
          "Process: the analysis and first drafts were produced by an AI system with automated reviews and no human expert review before submission; the authors have re-examined the results, and the code and data are available for independent replication."]:
    p = doc.add_paragraph(cite_sub(t), style="List Bullet"); p.paragraph_format.space_after = Pt(3)
H("Conclusions")
P("Acceptance that is unusually fast relative to the same journal and year is associated with later retraction for peer-review-related reasons, with a graded relation, specificity to such reasons and robustness to finer strata, mass-retraction events and the removal of the journals with most events, but the association varies widely between journals and years and has weakened for recent articles. As a screen it is weak because most flagged articles are not retracted. Journal-relative timing may help editorial offices prioritise groups of articles for review; it must not be used to judge individual papers, and any rule should be re-estimated routinely.")
